    "ExplanationCorrect": "Diana should recommend immediate replacement (Option 1) because the quantitative analysis demonstrates that the annual operational savings exceed the financing cost, while the qualitative factors make deferral and outsourcing unacceptable. Annual savings: maintenance reduction of $94,000 ($112,000 to $18,000) plus downtime recovery of $256,500 ($285,000 × 90%) = $350,500. The five-year equipment loan at 6.8% requires annual payments of approximately $578,000 on $2,400,000 — but this is the full debt service, not the incremental cost. The relevant comparison is the $350,500 in annual operational savings versus the incremental financing cost of the new debt ($2,400,000 × 6.8% ≈ $163,200 in first-year interest). With net annual benefit of approximately $187,300 before considering the tax shield on depreciation and interest, the replacement is financially justified. The 1.82:1 debt-to-equity ratio remains 0.18 under the 2.0:1 covenant limit — adequate headroom given Fenwick's stable operating history. Option 2 (predictive maintenance bridge) is merely deferring an inevitable capital decision while continuing to incur above-normal maintenance and downtime risk. The $285,000 in downtime losses last year alone exceeds the annual cost of the predictive-maintenance program — and downtime costs are trending upward as the machine ages. Option 3 (outsourcing) surrenders quality control over a mission-critical process and eliminates the process-innovation learning that comes from operating the equipment internally. Under CMA Part 1 capital budgeting, the capital expenditure budget exists to plan and control long-term investments — deferral and outsourcing are operational tactics that treat the symptom (aging equipment) while avoiding the capital decision the capex budget exists to make. A common exam trap is to confuse the full debt service payment with the incremental cost of financing, overstating the financial burden of replacement.",
