    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option 2 (predictive-maintenance bridge) is superficially attractive — it defers the $2.4 million capital outlay and preserves near-term cash. However, deferral is not a strategy for mission-critical equipment whose failure cost ($285,000 last year and rising) already exceeds the annual cost of predictive maintenance ($145,000). The three-year useful-life extension is an estimate based on a machine that has already shown accelerating deterioration — the $60,000 sensor investment does nothing to reverse the underlying mechanical wear. More importantly, deferral does not eliminate the capital need; it postpones it to a future period when interest rates may be higher and the equipment may have failed catastrophically, forcing an emergency replacement at premium pricing and with lost production during the procurement lead time. Under CMA Part 1, the capital expenditure budget is the planning tool for long-term asset decisions — if the analysis shows replacement is justified now, deferring to an arbitrary three-year horizon is not a capital budgeting decision, it is a gamble on equipment reliability. A candidate selecting this option may have prioritized near-term cash conservation over total cost of ownership.",
